\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 It was hard to say who was the more furious: the CEO; the Director of Moral Observance; the Director of Freedom; the Commissioner of Elvi Rights; the Refrigeration Ombudsman; or the President of the Humpty Doo Tank Battle Reenactment Club. Fortunately, the tank-enthusiast crocodile-rights-wrestler lacked the security clearance to come within three local governments around Parliament Market.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 In a fortress of neutral corporate chic, an expensive oval table for twenty occupied most of the polished space. Bleary-bright lighting made everything look as two-dimensional as folded construction paper. Expansive artworks\'97random geometry and beige aggression, insured for enough to buy a basketball team or a minor dictatorship\'97clung to reinforced greenish-beige walls as cheerful as the inside of a Tupperware lid from the time of the McMahon government.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Attendees sat upright in ergonomic status-affirming leather chairs, drumming the table with either manicured nails or, in one case, an ornate platinum key to the executive toilet, which the Director of Moral Observance drum-tapped on the table with the kind of casual entitlement that could only come from generations of inherited amenities.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>At the head, a media staffer played the package: the attack on Truth Central\'97PAPER\'92s pride and joy, now so much busted concrete and brickwork.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Tanks,\'94 spat the Director of Defence. \'93Every contingency\'97air raids, hackers, chem-bio, circus troupes\'97nobody ran a model for tanks.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Why not?\'94 asked Dunhilton, lacing the words with entitled bile.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93We\'92re an island. The idea of someone rolling tanks right up to our door was considered only fractionally more likely than submarines crossing the Western Desert.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chuckles circled the room. Only a failed African state kept submarines now, and their main use was piracy\'97or at best, novelty catering.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93And yet the tanks rolled,\'94 observed Moral Observance, sipping green tea and Benedictine with deliberate piety.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93They were our tanks,\'94 Gerry pointed out.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Defence bristled. \'93Not true.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93They were ordered by Defence, manufactured here,\'94 Gerry insisted, unmoved.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Never delivered,\'94 Defence retorted.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Silence frostbit the room. No one mentioned the granny in the room\'97Eloise Shakti, not present, but as impossible to ignore as a cockroach in a cream bun.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Since when do we blame the arms dealer for an uprising?\'94 The Commissioner of Elvi Rights\'92 role was, naturally, to ensure the Elvi had no rights at all.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93No one does,\'94 said Moral Observance, \'93Weapons are just business.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Everyone exhaled relief and ostentatious agreement\'97everyone, except Elvi Rights.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton turned, gaze transparent as clingfilm, to the Director of Freedom\'97keeper of national intelligence, including the famously lethal PAPER, the agency whose motto was 
\f2\i Figere Ante Alios Figere Nos
\f1\i0  (\'93Fix others before they fix us\'94) and was possibly the most lethally paranoid agency since the Mickey Mouse Club. \'93Are we certain the attack on the Truth facility was just about Anthony Chantara?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Freedom shrugged, stone-faced. \'93Besides Chantara, the insurgents shot up screens, a few toilets, some chandeliers...\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Chandeliers?\'94 barked Dunhilton.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Freedom slid a single-page report across. \'93See for yourself.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton squinted at the document. \'93\'91In the name of the Holy Momma, we\'92ve come to rescue you\'92? Is that all they said?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A collective chill, except for Elvi Rights, who nearly vibrated with schadenfreude.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93That\'92s all, and no other assets taken,\'94 Freedom concluded. \'93They withdrew after getting Chantara.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Elvi Rights, whose sole Key Performance Insinuator was the containment of the rights she was sworn to uphold, could not contain herself. \'93It is as we feared since the emancipation of the ELVIS units.\'94 She flipped through some documents before her and found a thin pamphlet which she unfolded. \'93I have here a Warranty Card for Grayling Corporation\'92s Entertainment Lifestyle Vocationally Integrated Servant units. It says, in part\'85\'94 she ran her finger down the pamphlet until she found her place and began to read aloud.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93
\f2\i This Warranty is immediately null and void, and the ELVIS shall be immediately reclaimable by GRAYLING on commission of any of the following violations: Feeding the ELVIS after midnight; Feeding the ELVIS bacon fat and coffee grounds; allowing the ELVIS to inhale or choke on rhinestones; Exposing the ELVIS\'97\'94\

\f1\i0 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Just get to the point; we know where you\'92re going,\'94 the Director of Corrections interjected.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93
\f2\i \'97to amphetamine, crystal methamphetamine, cocaine, or stimulant patch; Exposing the ELVIS\'97
\f1\i0 ah, here we are. 
\f2\i Exposing the ELVIS to religion or religious memes or icons including but not limited to religious texts, psalms, hymns, dashboard figurines, tacky paintings of a blue-eyed Jesus, or\'85\'94 
\f1\i0  and here she slowed down and raised her voice, 
\f2\i \'93\'85gospel music of any kind.\'94 
\f1\i0 She slapped the table as if saying 
\f2\i Case Closed
\f1\i0 .\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Just get to the point,\'94 Corrections snapped.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Elvi Rights slapped the card. \'93It means this Holy Momma, in whose name Truth was attacked, has voided the warranty on every ELVIS in the attack.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The room gasped: warranty malfeasance was a crime nearly as grave as government deficit spending, and was punishable by forfeiture all one\'92s worldly goods, both white and electrical. Unless, of course, you were Airborne with connections.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Jesus Taxing Christ! This Holy Momma could start a cult in the Ghetto,\'94 someone muttered\'97by this point, who could say who. The rest of the room degenerated into chaos.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The Ghetto? It could be the Pale!\'94 another yelped.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The whole country!\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Voices jangled until Agent Jones, who\'92d been lurking among the art installations, stepped forward, stole a pencil, snapped it like a neck, and slammed it on the table.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Silence.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93We must consider the worst-case scenario,\'94 Dunhilton pronounced grandly, \'93the entire Elvi population contaminated by religion.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93A religion other than Latter Day Capitalism,\'94 Moral Observance clarified.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Everyone solemnly laid a hand over their heart, covering the breast pocket where they kept their Coin of Allegiance.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I move for emergency powers under the Grayling Entertainment Units Emancipation Contract. Objections?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Tax, no!\'94 Elvi Rights crowed, then collected herself. \'93That is, given the unprecedented circumstances, I will refrain from exercising my powers to oppose.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Good,\'94 said the CEO. \'93Options?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Concentration camps,\'94 Corrections suggested, budget-raising already in mind.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93We can\'92t imprison them all,\'94 Gerry said, matter-of-fact. \'93We don\'92t have the resources.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I meant off-shore, of course,\'94 Corrections negotiated. \'93I have a portfolio of health services orgs who are busting to branch out into prisons, and would be willing to exact unconscionably inflated fees, due to the range of consultants and retainers they\'92d need to ensure an acceptable overrun of implementation.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Several players at the table began counting beach homes in their heads.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Just for the record,\'94 Elvi Rights chimed in, \'93I oppose, but perhaps the simplest answer is to simply wipe them out?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93We could finally start redeveloping the Ghetto,\'94 said Housing.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Other attendees refocused on inner high-rise fantasies.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93We can\'92t just go exterminating them,\'94 said Tourism, alarmed by logistical loss.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93If we exterminate them, we might make martyrs\'97export this Holy Momma faith no matter what we do.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93So stage a tragedy. Blame the Hand of Homer,\'94 Gerry suggested.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Freedom exchanged a glance with Gerry.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Plans?\'94 challenged the CEO.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Gerry spun a dossier and slid it down the table; Dunhilton clamped it instantly with his hand, ordering, \'93Everyone out.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Compliance, grudges, and mental calculations for new alibis rippled through the room as all but Gerry and Freedom filed out.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You too,\'94 Dunhilton directed at his chief security man.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Freedom glared at Gerry and smirked; knowing Gerry was a clandestine PAPER boy, he savoured the thought of debriefing him soon. He swore under his breath to make it thoroughly memorable.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>As the doors thudded shut, it was hard to say whose fury burned hottest, but history, as ever, would put the shortest odds on Agent Jones in a black suit and tie, and the Refrigeration Ombudsman.
